1. Kd4 {The king, at his queen's fourth square.} Kf5 {The king, at his bishop's fourth square.}
2. Ke3 {The king, at his third square.} Ke6 {The king, at his third square. (a) NOTE: Had he
played his king to your king's knight's fourth square, he would likewise lose the game.} 3. Ke4
{The king, at his fourth square.} Kd6 {The king, at his queen's third square. (b) NOTE: Had he
played his king to his second square, or to his queen's second square, you must have advanced
your king opposite to his, in order to effect the taking of his pawn.} 4. f5 {The king's
bishop's pawn, one move.} gxf5+ {The pawn takes the pawn. (c) NOTE: He could have declined
taking your pawn; but that other way of playing will be found in our next back-game.} 5. Kxf5
{The king retakes the pawn.} Ke7 {The king, at his second square.} 6. Kg6 {The king, at the
adverse king's knight's third square.} Kf8 {The king, at his bishop's square.} 7. Kh7 {The king,
at the adverse king's rook's second square, and will win. (d) NOTE: As often as the king is
before a pawn, the adversary cannot hinder you from making a queen.} *
